silm:instances_papers_00_whitepaper_sections_06_closure_concrete_anchor
    a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "ClosureConcreteAnchorExample" ;
    silm:hasExample "In 'ClosureConcreteAnchor', the bare-symbol→anchor table grounds the abstract symbols in the Tartu-Animal-Clinic / Mittens-2026-05-18 domain story. For example, TartuClinicCompleteSystem: the complete Tartu Animal Clinic record and publication system that simultaneously enforces the five spine theorems (monad algebra, twelve-leg cocone, four-gate publish law, multi-view rendering contract, sous observation boundary); also, MonadAlgebraContract: the Tartu Clinic's monad algebra structure (ingest unit, publish multiplication, bind associativity) that makes the EncyclopediaEntry type a free-monad algebra; also, TwelveLegMittensSystem: the Tartu Clinic's twelve-typed-observation system for Mittens (VetRecord_77, MicrochipID_44, BodyMassQuality, WeightMeas_3450g, Value_3450, Unit_grams, PetRole, SleepProcess_7h, SHACLPassReport_77, AuditTrail_77, DomesticCat, FelisCatusTSN183798)." .

silm:instances_papers_00_whitepaper_sections_06_closure_decomposition_step__step_spine_colimit_spinecolimit
    a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "SpineColimit is the colimit of the five spine theorems T1-T5 assembled as a compatible cocone. It is not any single theorem but the universal object that all five theories point to and that all 41 sibling papers inherit." .

silm:instances_papers_00_whitepaper_sections_06_closure_decomposition_step__step_%3E%3E%3D_monadic_bind__free_monad_composition
    a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label ">>= is the monadic bind of the free monad: T1 >>= T2 means 'use the output of T1 (the free-monad algebra structure) as the context for T2 (the twelve-leg cocone theorem)'. The bind is associative: ((T1 >>= T2) >>= T3) equals T1 >>= (T2 >>= T3) by the free-monad associativity law." .

silm:instances_papers_00_whitepaper_sections_06_closure_decomposition_step__step_free_monad_associativity_joc_%C2%A720_1
    a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The free-monad associativity law (JoC §20.1): ((T >>= U) >>= V) = T >>= (U >>= V). This means the five-theorem composition produces the same SpineColimit regardless of how the agent passes are grouped. The closure theorem relies on this law to establish that the five theorems compose into a single well-defined spine colimit." .

silm:instances_papers_00_whitepaper_sections_06_closure_decomposition_step__step_cheese_trap_faces_closed_by_closure_theorem
    a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The closure theorem closes the following ten cheese-trap faces: Face 1 (Mittens = catus), Face 2 (TSN 183798 = Mittens), Face 3 (DomesticCat = any catus row), Face 4 (VetRecord_77 = Mittens), Face 5 (Value_3450 = BodyMassQuality), Face 6 (SHACLPassReport_77 = TartuClinicIntakeForm), Face 7 (VetCarePlanPDF_77 = VetCarePlanPDF_77 the claim), Face 8 (FelisCatusTSN183798 = the species category), Face 9 (PetRole = Mittens), Face 10 (SleepProcess_7h = Mittens). Each face is closed by one of the five spine theorems; the closure theorem names the closing theorem for each face." .

silm:instances_papers_00_whitepaper_sections_06_closure_decomposition_step__step_yoneda_inheritance_of_the_closure_theorem
    a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "By Yoneda naturality, the closure theorem is inherited by every downstream paper via the Chapter 12 spine-inheritance contracts. A downstream paper that cites any of the five spine theorems inherits the full closure guarantee: if T2 (twelve-leg cocone) is inherited, then Face 1-3 closures are inherited for free; if T3 (gate law) is inherited, then Faces 6-7 closures are inherited for free." .

silm:instances_papers_00_whitepaper_sections_06_closure_decomposition_step__step_closure_theorem_recap
    a owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The five spine theorems (free-monad thesis, twelve-leg Mittens cocone, Node-or-Arrow gate, multi-view agreement, sous Yoneda law) compose associatively via the free-monad bind into SpineColimit; the closure theorem closes all ten cheese-trap faces; every sibling paper inherits the closure via Yoneda naturality." .

